put, error messages, and exit code properties:map[command:map[description:The command that was executed example:ls -la type:string] error:map[description:Error message if the command failed (only present if there was an error) example:exit status 127 type:string] exit_code:map[description:Exit code of the command (only present if the command failed) example:%!s(int=127) type:integer] output:map[description:Standard output and error from the command example:total 24
drwxr-xr-x 5 user group 4096 Jan 15 10:30 . type:string] success:map[description:Whether the command executed successfully example:%!s(bool=true) type:boolean]] type:object]] map[description:
	Writes a chunk of content to a file at a specific offset.
	
	This tool is designed for writing large files that exceed the token limit.
	You should break down large content into smaller chunks and call this tool
	multiple times, each with a different offset.
	
	IMPORTANT: 
	- The first call should have offset = 0
	- Subsequent calls should have offset equal to total size of previously written content
	- The last chunk should have is_last = true
	- If is_last is true, file will be truncated at current offset + content length
	- CRITICAL: Each chunk must not exceed 100 characters to ensure reliable processing
	
	To use this tool:
	1. Provide the actual filename, content, offset, and is_last values
	2. Do NOT provide the parameter schema/definition
	3. Call this tool multiple times for large files, each time with the next offset
	
	Args (example values):
	{
	  "filename": "large_file.txt",
	  "content": "First part of the content...",
	  "offset": 0,
	  "is_last": false
	}
	
	Example usage for writing a large file:
	1. First chunk:
	   write_file_chunk({
	     "filename": "large_file.txt",
	     "content": "First part of the content...",
	     "offset": 0,
	     "is_last": false
	   })
	   
	2. Second chunk:
	   write_file_chunk({
	     "filename": "large_file.txt",
	     "content": "Second part of the content...",
	     "offset": 1024,
	     "is_last": false
	   })
	   
	3. Last chunk:
	   write_file_chunk({
	     "filename": "large_file.txt",
	     "content": "Last part of the content...",
	     "offset": 2048,
	     "is_last": true
	   })
	   
	Note: When writing large files, ensure each chunk is within the 100 character limit
	for reliable processing. If a chunk exceeds this limit, it may be truncated or fail.
	 name:write_file_chunk parameters:map[properties:map[content:map[description:The content chunk to write to the file. type:string] filename:map[description:The path to the file. Can be an absolute path or a relative path. type:string] is_last:map[description:True if this is the last chunk, false otherwise. type:boolean] offset:map[description:The byte offset in the file where this chunk should be written. type:integer]] required:[filename content offset is_last] type:object] results:map[description:Result of the file write operation properties:map[bytes_written:map[description:Number of bytes written in this chunk example:%!s(int=1024) type:integer] filename:map[description:The absolute path of the file that was written to example:/path/to/large_file.txt type:string] is_last:map[description:Indicates whether this was the last chunk to write example:%!s(bool=false) type:boolean] offset:map[description:The offset in the file where the chunk was written example:%!s(int=0) type:integer] success:map[description:Indicates whether the write operation was successful example:%!s(bool=true) type:boolean]] type:object]] map[description:
	Write content to a file with automatic structure design.
	MUST ensure the content is structured and readable.
	MUST ensure Args is json formatted.
	
	
	You MUST NOT write raw or unstructured text.
	
	Before writing:
	1. Understand the purpose of the content
	2. Design a clear structure
	3. Format using Markdown
	
	You are responsible for:
	- Content organization
	- Readability
	- Proper formatting
	
	Bad example (DO NOT DO):
	- Plain paragraphs without structure
	
	Good example:
	- Clear headings
	- Bullet points
	- Logical sections
	
	If the user gives rough ideas, you MUST refine and structure them.
	
	Args:
	{
	  "path": "file path",
	  "content": "structured markdown content"
	}
	 name:file_write parameters:map[properties:map[content:map[description:The content to write to the file. This can be any text content. type:string] path:map[description:The path to the file. Can be an absolute path (e.g., 'C:\temp\file.txt' on Windows or '/tmp/file.txt' on Unix) or a relative path (e.g., './data/file.txt'). Relative paths are resolved from the current working directory. type:string]] required:[path content] type:object] results:map[description:A message indicating the result of the file write operation. type:string]] map[description:Converts a location name (city, address, landmark) into latitude and longitude coordinates. Input should be a place name like 'Beijing', 'New York', or 'Eiffel Tower'. Returns the coordinates and additional location details. name:geocoding parameters:map[properties:map[location:map[description:The location name to geocode. Must use 'location' as the parameter name. Examples: 'Beijing', 'Times Square', 'Tokyo Tower' type:string]] required:[location] type:object] results:map[description:Geocoding tool to convert a location name into latitude and longitude coordinates properties:map[latitude:map[description:The latitude coordinate of the location example:39.9042 type:string] longitude:map[description:The longitude coordinate of the location example:116.4074 type:string]] type:object]] map[description:Get weather and forecast information for a specific location. This tool provides comprehensive weather data including temperature, humidity, wind speed, precipitation probability, and weather conditions. Use this tool when user asks about weather, temperature, or forecast for any location. Requires latitude and longitude coordinates of the location. name:query_weather parameters:map[properties:map[days:map[description:Number of days to forecast (default: 7, max: 16) type:integer] latitude:map[description:The latitude coordinate of the location (e.g., 39.9042 for Beijing) type:string] longitude:map[description:The longitude coordinate of the location (e.g., 116.4074 for Beijing) type:string]] required:[latitude longitude] type:object] results:map[description:Weather forecast data with daily breakdown and insights properties:map[daily_forecast:map[description:Array of daily weather forecasts items:map[properties:map[date:map[description:Date in YYYY-MM-DD format example:2024-01-15 type:string] insights:map[description:Daily weather insights and recommendations example:[Temperature rising High chance of rain - consider carrying an umbrella] items:map[type:string] type:array] summary:map[properties:map[condition:map[description:Primary weather condition example:Partly cloudy type:string] max_precip_prob:map[description:Maximum precipitation probability (0-100) example:%!s(int=30) type:integer] max_wind_speed:map[description:Maximum wind speed in km/h example:%!s(float64=15.5) type:number] temperature_range:map[description:Temperature range in Celsius example:15.5°C ~ 22.3°C type:string] trend:map[description:Temperature trend (warming/cooling/stable) example:warming type:string]] type:object]] type:object] type:array] forecast_days:map[description:Number of days in the forecast (1-16) example:%!s(int=3) type:integer] overall_insights:map[description:Overall weather trends and patterns for the forecast period example:[Overall temperature trend: Warming up over the forecast period Rain expected on 2 day(s) during the forecast period] items:map[type:string] type:array]] type:object]] map[description:Calculates time by adding or subtracting duration from current time. Args format: 'operation:duration' where operation is 'add' or 'subtract', and duration is like '1h30m' (hours and minutes) name:calculate_time parameters:map[properties:map[duration:map[description:The duration to add or subtract, e.g., '1h30m' for 1 hour and 30 minutes type:string] operation:map[description:The operation to perform: 'add' or 'subtract' type:string]] required:[operation duration] type:object] results:map[description:Calculated time result properties:map[calculated_time:map[description:The calculated time in RFC3339 format example:2024-01-15T10:30:00+08:00 type:string]] type:object]] map[description:Manage files and folders inside the app's document library root (a folder named "workspace" under the app working directory). All paths are relative to that root — use forward slashes (e.g. "小说/三侠五义/第一回.md"). Safe: cannot escape outside the library.

Use for: creating novel/project folders, chapter files, notes trees. Combine with operation write_file for content. Prefer mkdir before write_file when the parent folder does not exist.

Operations:
- list_dir: list entries in "path" (default "").
- mkdir: create directory "path" (and parents).
- write_file: overwrite file at "path" with "content" (UTF-8 text).
- delete: remove file or empty directory; set recursive_delete true to remove a non-empty directory tree.
- rename: move/rename from "path" to "path_to".

Example: user wants 小说/三侠五义 with chapters — mkdir "小说", mkdir "小说/三侠五义", write_file "小说/三侠五义/第一回.md" with content, etc. name:library_fs parameters:map[properties:map[content:map[description:For write_file: full file content (overwrite). type:string] operation:map[description:Action to perform. enum:[list_dir mkdir write_file delete rename] type:string] path:map[description:Relative path inside the library (e.g. 小说/三侠五义). Use "" for list_dir root. type:string] path_to:map[description:For rename: new relative path inside the library. type:string] recursive_delete:map[description:For delete: if true, remove directories with all contents. Default false. type:boolean]] required:[operation] type:object] results:map[description:Result message or JSON (list_dir). type:string]] map[description:Write or update the user's in-app memo / diary file (Markdown). Same storage as the 备忘录 panel (data/memo.md).
Use for travel plans, todos, notes the user asked to keep. Prefer structured Markdown (headings, lists).
For the sidebar calendar "memo dot", include a date like YYYY-MM-DD in section_title or in the first lines of content (or use default append heading which includes the date).
mode "append" adds a new dated section; "replace" overwrites the whole memo (use only when the user explicitly wants to clear or replace everything).
Args JSON: {"content": "...", "mode": "append"|"replace", "section_title": "optional heading for append"} name:memo_write parameters:map[properties:map[content:map[description:Markdown text to save. type:string] mode:map[description:append (default): add a section; replace: overwrite entire memo. enum:[append replace] type:string] section_title:map[description:Optional section heading when mode is append; default is local date-time. type:string]] required:[content] type:object] results:map[description:Confirmation with memo file path. type:string]]]
